**SQL injection.** `req.params.id` injected directly into query string. Attacker sends `1 OR 1=1` → dumps all users. Sends `1; DROP TABLE users` → data loss.

**Fix — parameterized query:**

```js
app.get('/api/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [req.params.id])
    .then(user => {
      if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });
      res.json(user);
    })
    .catch(() => res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' }));
});
```

**Other issues:**

| Issue | Risk | Fix |
|-------|------|-----|
| No input validation | Non-integer id still hits DB | Validate `id` is integer before query |
| No error handling | DB errors leak stack traces | Add `.catch()` — return generic error |
| `SELECT *` | Exposes all columns incl. password hashes | Enumerate only needed columns |
| No auth check | Any caller reads any user | Add auth middleware, check caller owns resource |

**Priority:** SQL injection is critical — fix first. Parameterized queries fix it completely regardless of input.
